        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.0,
            0.069,
            0.262,
            0.098
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "I Overview: An Attempt to Classify"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.046,
            0.151,
            0.367,
            0.204
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thinking rationally"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.25,
            0.827,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "\u0000 Aristotle made first attempt to harnes s “right thinking” . Famous example : “S ocrates is a man ; all men are mortal ; therefore S ocrates is mortal .”"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.404,
            0.796,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Formal logic (late nineteenth, early twentieth centuries) provides a precise notation of statements of all kinds of things and relations between them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.558,
            0.812,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Programs that can find the solution to a logical problem if one exists have been around since 1 965 . Given enough memory and time, they should be able to solve everything."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.068,
            0.714,
            0.206,
            0.747
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "\u0000 Problems :"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.768,
            0.757,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "– not everything can be formalized, e.g. uncertain knowledge"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.821,
            0.804,
            0.905
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "– complexity of problems easily exhausts todays (and tomorrows) computational resources ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.0,
            0.954,
            0.351,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "IS 675 Peter ´ Molnar´ , 2003 (all rights reserved)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.829,
            0.955,
            0.885,
            0.98
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Slide 7"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.0,
            0.069,
            0.262,
            0.098
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "I Overview: An Attempt to Classify"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.046,
            0.281,
            0.325,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Acting rationally"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.382,
            0.72,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "\u0000 “ . . . acting so as to achieve one ’ s goals , given one ’ s beliefs .”"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.45,
            0.542,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "\u0000 Obj ective : construction of rational "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "agents",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.516,
            0.824,
            0.651
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "AI as rational agent design is more general than the “laws of thought” approach; correct inference is useful but not necessary for achieving rationality."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.68,
            0.817,
            0.767
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "\u0000 In contrast to human-centered approaches , the standard of rationality is clearly defined and general."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.0,
            0.954,
            0.351,
            0.982
          ],
